 succeeded in 0ms:
# Chapter 5 — Ten

The Ironyard before the sweepers came was a different building from the one he fought in.

At night, with the fourteen lamps lit and three hundred people breathing in it, it was close and warm and loud, and the low ceiling seemed to sit on everybody's head. At first light it was cold, and the lamps were dark on their chains, and the only light came grey through the high north windows and lay on the stone in long pale bars. The stone itself looked older in that light. You could see the marks of whatever had stood on it when it was a foundry floor, the round worn places and the long scars, before anybody had thought of fighting on it at all.

Lira was there before him. She had chalked a cross on the floor of the alcove, a stride out from the straw post, and she was standing beside it with her staff across her shoulders and her hands hooked over it, like a farmer with a yoke.

"You land there," she said. "Every time. I want to be able to tell the difference between you landing somewhere new and you landing badly. I'll come from wherever I like."

"That's fair."

"I didn't ask if it was fair." She looked at his thigh, which he had wrapped, and then at his face, which he had not. "Ten, you said. Not eleven because you've had a good idea on the ninth."

"Ten."

"And I count."

"You count."

She took the staff off her shoulders and turned it once in her hands, and went back three paces, and set herself. Not in her ordinary stance. In the one she used for the girls from the wall when she wanted to show them what it looked like to mean something: lower, heavier, the staff held short. It was the stance of a fighter who intends to hit you hard and has decided not to feel sorry about it until afterward. He had seen it from the rope a hundred times. He had very seldom seen it from in front.

"Go when you like," said Lira.

He went from the far side of the alcove, at a run, the way he would come off a feint in a bout, and dropped his left hip and held his breath and let the floor go short, and was on the chalk cross, and locked.

She was already moving. She had started before he left the ground, because she knew where the cross was, and the staff came round at his collarbone on a long flat line, and he stood on the chalk and watched it come the whole way. He saw her back foot turn and her weight go over it. He saw her shoulder roll into the line. He saw the end of the staff, quite clearly, with a nick in it from some morning years ago, and knew to the width of a finger where it would land.

"One," said Lira, as it landed, and then, without a pause: "*one-and-two-and*—" and the lock let go of him on the second *and*, and he staggered, and the collarbone began to sing.

"That's where it lets go," she said. "Every time I've ever watched it. Two *ands*. You want to do it again?"

"That's what we're here for."

The second time she came from his right, low, at the ribs. He landed. He locked. He watched the staff come round under his guard and could have drawn its path on a slate. It hit him in the side where Darrow had built his long bruise in the spring, which had healed months ago and seemed nonetheless to remember being asked, and Lira's voice said "*one-and-two-and*," and on the second *and* he could move again.

He stood with his hand on his side and breathed. So much for the baseline. It was what he had expected: the lock came, it held, it let go at the same place in her count, and while it held he could see everything and do nothing.

"Two," said Lira. "Both the same. Two *ands*."

"I know. I could hear you."

"Could you see it coming?"

"All of it. Everything." He straightened. "I could see the nick in your staff."

She looked down at the end of her staff, surprised, and found the nick with her thumb, and looked back at him, and something went across her face that was not quite worry.

"Three," she said. "Go on."

---

The third time he tried landing soft.

It was the first of his ideas, and he had liked it best in the dark the night before, lying on his back with his hip aching. If the lock came from the landing, perhaps it came from landing *hard*: the weight thumping back into the heels all at once, as he always felt it, like a sack off a cart. If he landed with his knees already loose, already bent, ready to give, perhaps the half-breath would have nowhere to catch.

He came off the far wall and dropped and went and held his breath and came down on the chalk with his knees soft as a cat's.

And locked, exactly as hard, with his knees bent.

It was worse, if anything. A man locked upright could at least be hit standing. A man locked with his knees bent was a man in a chair with no chair under him. Lira's staff came across at his shoulder and he watched it come from a crouch and could do nothing about it, and it knocked him half round, and he heard her say "*two-and*" from what seemed a long way off, and then he was free and sitting down on the stone.

"Three," said Lira. "Same."

He got up. "Four. Shorter."

That was the second idea. If the lock was the end of the burst, then a shorter burst ended sooner, and if it ended sooner, perhaps he would be through the lock and out of it before the staff could finish its trip. He took the run again and dropped and went, and this time he cut the burst off early, threw his weight down before the floor had finished going short, so that he landed a long pace in front of the chalk instead of on it.

Lira's staff went through the air behind him, where the chalk was, and touched nothing.

He stood there locked in the wrong place, with his heart going, and for a moment he thought he had found it.

"*Two-and*," said Lira, and he was free, and he turned round and saw her face, and she was not pleased. She was standing with the staff at the end of its swing and her eyes on his feet.

"That missed because you were somewhere I didn't expect," she said. "Not because you were out of it. Look where you are."

He looked. He had cut the burst short, and so he had not gone as far, and so he was standing a long pace closer to her than the chalk was. Close enough that she could have reached him without stepping at all if she had known he would be there.

"It was the same length," said Lira. "The lock. I counted. Two *ands*. You only moved it nearer to me."

"Four," he said. "Five. Breath."

Breath was the third idea, and the one he had least hope for and most curiosity about. The burst would not go on a moving breath; he held it in, every time, half a lungful. Perhaps the lock was only the breath catching up, and if he let it out as he landed, instead of after, he would let the lock out with it.

He landed and let his breath go in a long rush, and the lock did not care. He stood on the chalk with his lungs empty and his body as fixed as a post driven into a riverbed, and Lira hit him across the back of the shoulders, not as hard as before, and he could not even grunt, because there was nothing in him to grunt with. On the second *and* he folded over and pulled in air like a man coming up out of a pond.

"That was stupid," he said, when he could.

"I didn't like to say." She was watching him with her head on one side. "Five. All the same length. Every one."

"Six. The other foot."

It was the last of the ideas he had brought with him. The lock held him rooted to both feet; but if, in the air, he set his weight a little more onto his right foot than his left, perhaps when the lock let go he would be already leaning, already half moved, and could push off it the instant it opened instead of a beat after.

He did. He came down onto the chalk with his weight on the right, and the lock took him, and held him, leaning, exactly as it had held him standing and crouching and empty of breath. And Lira, who had watched him lean, came in on his left, where the weight was not, and put the end of the staff into the outside of his left hip, where the burst had been charging him rent since the spring. The hip went white. On the second *and* he fell over sideways onto the stone, because the lean had nowhere left to go.

He lay on the stone with his hip going like a drum and studied the beams.

---

"Seven," he said to the beams.

"You're bleeding a bit," said Lira. "Your lip. Not much."

He had bitten it on the way down. He sat up and wiped it on his sleeve and looked at the faint mark, and did not think it counted as anything, and got up.

"Seven. Toward you."

She looked at him. "Toward me?"

"If I burst at you instead of away, I land inside your staff. You can't swing a long thing at somebody who's standing on your feet."

"You can't do anything at all when you're standing on my feet," said Lira. "You're locked."

"Then neither of us can do anything. That's better than me being hit."

She considered that, honestly, and he saw her decide that it was at least a new mistake, and set herself.

He went at her. The floor went short, and the half body's width that usually took him round her took him straight into her instead, and he landed so close that his chest bumped her staff where she held it across herself, and locked there. She had been ready for him to land somewhere. She had not been ready for him to land on her. She tried to step back to get room to swing and found his foot on hers, and pushed at him with the staff, and he could not move to be pushed, and the two of them leaned on each other like two drunk men holding up a wall. On the second *and* the lock let go and his weight came off her foot all at once, and she went backward and he went forward, and they both sat down hard on the stone in a tangle of staff and knees.

There was a pause.

Then Lira began to laugh. She laughed with her head back against the straw post and her eyes shut, and he found that he was laughing too, though it hurt the collarbone and the side and the hip and the lip all at once, and for a little while they sat on the cold floor of the alcove in the grey light and could not stop.

"That's not a fix," she said at last, wiping her eyes. "That's a dance."

"It's not a hit, though."

"It's not anything. I'd have stepped back if you'd come from further off. Anybody would." She got up and held a hand down to him. "Seven. Two *ands*. I counted, even laughing."

He took her hand and came up. He was beginning to feel it now, the thing the bursts cost that was not in the hip or the breath: a hollowness behind his breastbone, as though somebody had scooped out a cupful of whatever was in there and not put anything back. Seven bursts in less than an hour. He had never spent seven in an hour in his life.

"Eight," he said. "Out of it."

"Out of what?"

"Out of the lock. With another burst. If the first one locks me, maybe a second one can carry me out."

Lira's face changed. "Cael."

"I want to know."

She did not argue. She set herself, and he went, and landed, and locked, and in the lock he reached for the burst again, the drop and the loosening, the way he reached for it a dozen times a morning; and it was not there. It was not refused; refused would have been something. It was as if he had put his hand into a pocket where he always kept a coin and found the pocket sewn shut. And yet something went out of him all the same, a little of the breath and a little of the hip, as though the reaching had been charged for even though nothing had come. Lira's staff came across at his collarbone, the same one, the bad one, and he watched it all the way in.

On the second *and* he went down onto one knee and stayed there.

"Eight," said Lira quietly. "Same length. Every one of them, the same length. I've counted it eight times with my own breath and it's never once been anything but two *ands*." She came and squatted on her heels just out of his reach, with the staff laid across her thighs. "You tried to make it shorter five ways. Soft, short, breath, lean, a second one. It didn't get shorter once. It's as long as it is. Arguing won't make it any shorter."

"I know."

"Do you?"

He did not answer that. He got up, slowly, and his head went light, and the alcove tipped a little and came back. He went to the far wall for the ninth because he did not know what else to do, and came off it, and dropped, and went; and somewhere in the burst his tired legs did not quite agree with his hip about where they were going. He came down crooked, half off the chalk, with his weight already toppling, and the lock caught him in the act of falling and held him there, tipped, like a picture of a boy falling over.

Lira had been coming. He saw the staff start. He saw her see him, and see what shape he was in, and take the strike back out of the air as though she had caught a cup knocked off a table.

The lock let go and he fell over properly.

"I don't hit people who are already on their way down," said Lira, standing over him. "There's no news in it. You knew how that one ended before I did."

He lay on his back on the stone with his arm over his eyes.

---

"That's nine," she said after a while.

She had sat down on the floor beside him, with her back against the straw post and her knees up and the staff across them. She did not say anything else, and she did not offer to help him up, and he understood that she was waiting.

"Nine in an hour," he said from under his arm. "I've never spent nine in a day."

"No."

"I can feel it." He could. The hollow behind his breastbone had widened, and it had a weight to it now, which made no sense, since it was an emptiness; it sat in his chest like a cold stone made of nothing. His hip throbbed with his heart. His breath kept wanting to stop on its own, halfway in, as though it had got the habit. "I said ten."

"You did say ten." Lira turned the staff over in her hands. "You get the tenth when you can tell me what it's for."

He took his arm off his eyes and looked at her.

"Five of those were you trying to make it shorter," she said, "and it didn't get shorter, and you knew by the third one it wouldn't, and you did two more anyway because you'd come with five ideas and you didn't want to waste any. The seventh made me laugh, which I'll give you. The ninth was your legs telling you something." She looked at him steadily. "I'll do the tenth. I'll do it as hard as the first. But not so you can lie on the floor afterward with a tenth bruise and a tenth *same length*. Tell me what it's for, and I'll give it you."

He lay there and thought about it, because she had asked it properly.

He had been asking the half-breath one question all morning, and all month, and if he was honest, all the time since she had first told him it was there. *How do I get out of you?* He had asked it nine ways now, and eight times it had given him the same answer, counted aloud in Lira's breath, and the ninth time it had not needed to.

But there was a thing he had said himself, after the first trial, which he had not listened to. *I could see the nick in your staff.* He had seen everything. Every time, from the first, while he stood there unable to move a finger, he had seen her back foot turn and her weight cross it and her shoulder come into line and the staff come round, all of it, whole, with nothing in the way. Nothing in the way, because there was nothing for him to do. No choice to make. No feint to watch for. No flinch. In half a breath of being a post, there was nothing in him at all except eyes.

He had been treating the lock like a cell he was shut in. It was also the only seat in the building with a perfect view.

"It's not a fault," he said slowly. "I've been writing it down as a fault. Faults you can train out, and I've just spent nine trying, and it won't go. It's the same length every time, like my arms are the same length every time. So it isn't a fault. It's a price." He sat up, carefully. "And if it's a price, it's buying something. Half a breath when I can't do anything except look. I've been spending it wanting out. What if I spend it reading? All of it. Whatever's coming at me, the whole strike, start to end, so that when it lets go I'm already moving, with the answer ready, instead of starting to think."

Lira was quiet for the space of three of her own breaths.

"And if what's coming is too big to stand up after?"

"Then it isn't a read. It's a present, and I'm lying on the floor holding it." There was no softer way to put that, so he did not try. "So it only works against something I can afford. One hit I've already priced. Anything bigger and I don't land near it."

She got up. She went back to her three paces and set herself in the heavy stance, the staff short.

"Ten," said Lira. "Don't do anything clever."

He went. The floor went short. He landed on the chalk and the lock took him.

And this time he did not want to be out of it. He let it hold him, and he gave it his eyes, and nothing else. He watched her back foot turn on the stone and knew from the angle of it which way she would come. He watched her weight go over the foot and knew how hard. He watched her shoulder roll and saw the line, a long one, right hand leading, the end of the staff travelling for the point of his left shoulder at a height and an angle he could put a number on. He put a number on it. *That one I can stand.* He paid for it in his head before it arrived, accepted it, set it down as spent. And then, with the rest of the half-breath, he looked past the strike to what came after it: her right arm at full stretch, her weight gone forward past her lead foot, and the recovery, the little step back she always took after a big swing, which he had watched her take four hundred times.

He loaded the answer. He did not move; he could not move. But he put everything in order, the way you set out tools on a bench before you start, so that the hand does not have to look for them.

On the second *and* the lock let go, and he was already going.

The staff took him on the point of the shoulder, full weight, an honest hit, and it turned him a quarter round. And in the same moment, inside the turn, his left hand was on her wrist where her arm was still at full stretch, and his right was at her elbow, and her recovery step, the one he had watched four hundred times, came back onto a foot that he had put his own foot beside. She went off balance. She grabbed his coat. The two of them went round together, staggering, half a turn on the chalk, holding on to each other, and stopped, panting, with the staff pinned between them and her wrist still in his hand and nobody, quite, on top.

They stood there. Her eyes were very wide.

"That's an exchange," said Lira. "If Vell had been here. That's not a hit for me, and it's not one for you. That's an exchange."

"Yes."

She let go of his coat. He let go of her wrist. She stood back and flexed the arm and looked at him.

"Tell me what you did."

"Nothing," said Cael. His shoulder was beginning to stiffen already, and he did not care. "That's the whole thing. I stopped trying to do anything. I let it be the length it is, and I looked."

He sat down on the stone, because his legs had decided, and she sat down beside him, and they leaned against the straw post together. After a while Lira held up her hand and counted it off on her fingers, as if she were going to chalk it on the canvas later, which she was.

"Six hits," she said. "One miss that was really a mistake. One dance. One I wouldn't. And an exchange." She folded the hand away. "And now you've got a rule, I suppose. You've got your rule face on."

"Here's the rule, and it starts now," said Cael. "The burst stays in my hip whenever somebody's close enough to hit me and I haven't already worked out what their one good hit would cost. Not once. Not because I'm winning, not because I'm frightened, not because it's the end of a long night and I want to go home. If I can't price it, I don't land near it."

"Good," said Lira. Then she looked at him sideways. "That's the most sensible thing you've said in a month, and it took you ten bruises."

---

He paid for it all the next day, and most of the day after.

He woke before light with the hollow behind his breastbone grown into something he could almost hold, a cold weight of nothing that sat on his chest like a cat. His breath kept stopping by itself halfway in. His left hip had set in the night into a single long line of ache from the crest of the bone to the inside of the knee, so that he went down the stairs one at a time with his hand on the wall. The collarbone had a mark on it the shape of a staff end. The shoulder would not lift above the level of his ear.

Lira took one look at him at the bottom of the stairs and sent him back up them.

"No alcove," she said. "No market. No bench. If I see you at the Ironyard I'll tell Vell you're unfit and she'll write it in her book, and then everybody will know." She put a cup of the house's tea into his hands, and a heel of bread. "Lie down and look at the ceiling. You're good at looking. Look at that."

He lay down and looked at the ceiling.

He had not known, until that day, how much of him was the bursts. He had thought of them as a thing he did with his hip, as a man thinks of whistling as a thing he does with his mouth; but the hollow behind his breastbone did not feel like the hip, or the breath. It felt like the place he went to when the floor went short, the dark place behind the bone where the notices came, and it felt as though he had gone to it too often in too short a time and worn a hole in the floor of it. By the middle of the afternoon he could not have called the burst if his life had hung on it. He knew, without trying, that it was not there to call. It would come back. He was nearly sure it would come back. He lay on his bed and was nearly sure for several hours.

In the late afternoon the heavyset man knocked on the open door, which he had never done before, and stood in it holding a letter out at arm's length, as if it might go off.

"Came on the east cart," he said. "Your name on it. Sealed." He looked at the bed and the boy on it with the same face he used for weather. "Win?"

"Something like."

"Hm," said the heavyset man, and gave him the letter, and went away down the stairs, and Cael heard him say to someone in the hall, "He's lying down. Fell off something, by the look of him."

It was Hesk's hand, and Hesk's seal, the plain one with the bracket on it. He broke it lying on his back and held the page up against the light from the window.

*Cael,*

*Yours came Tuesday. I've read it three times, which you'll say is two more than it needed, and you'd be right.*

*The workshop's quiet. I've a commission for a set of bearing housings from the mill at the bottom of Fen Street that I don't much like the look of; their drawings are wrong in two places and they won't hear it. I'll make them right and charge them for it and they'll thank me at midwinter when the wheel doesn't seize. The grey dog has taken to sleeping on the step. I don't feed him. He doesn't believe me.*

*You wrote that there's a half-breath after the thing you do, where you can't move. You wrote that you mean to find out what it's worth.*

*I've spent my whole working life with joints that won't move, and I'll tell you the one thing I know about them, for whatever it's worth to you over there. A joint that won't move isn't a bad joint. It's a fixed one, and a fixed joint is the thing you measure everything else from. You can't take a true reading off a part that wanders. You take it off the part that stays put.*

*Find out what it's worth. Then tell me. I'll want to know.*

*— H.*

He read it twice, and then he lay with it on his chest, on top of the cold weight of nothing, and looked at the ceiling as Lira had told him, and found that he was smiling at it.

*You take it off the part that stays put.*

He had found that out yesterday, on his back on a stone floor, at the cost of nine bursts and six hits and a lip. Hesk had known it all along, from a lifetime of housings, and had sent it over the hills in a letter that had been on the road for days, so that it arrived the afternoon after Cael had paid for it himself. He did not mind. It was a strange kind of comfort to have paid full price for a thing and then find it written down, word for word, in a hand he loved.

Through the open door between the rooms he heard Lira come in, and drop her staff in the corner, and stop.

"You're smiling at the ceiling," she said.

"Hesk wrote."

"What did he say?"

"That I was right," said Cael. "A day late. In his own words." He held the page up so that she could take it if she wanted. "He says a part that won't move is the part you measure everything else from."

Lira came and took the letter and read it standing in the doorway, and then read it again, and gave it back.

"I'd like to meet him," she said, which she said every time.

exec
