editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-01.md:119:It was a standard purse for an assessed bout: twelve marks between the two of them, put up before the card. Eight went to the winner and three to the loser and one to the ledger, and Vell had once explained the three to him, unasked, as the most important figure in the whole circuit. A beaten fighter who went home with something in his pocket came back the next month to try again. A beaten fighter who went home with nothing went somewhere worse, where nobody kept a book, and sooner or later somebody there did not get up. The one paid for Vell's ink and Vell's evenings and the plain fact of a record. Dace took nothing from the purse at all, only his share of the door, and Cael had marked that in his first month as the cleverest thing in the building: the man who chose who fought whom had no money riding on who won.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-01.md:121:Eight marks, then. Rent on the two back rooms was nine marks a month for the pair. One good win a month kept a roof on them; two kept the roof on and the stove lit and the paper stall paid and something put by. Hesk's pouch, the old leather one with the drawstring, sat on the shelf at home almost as full as the day it had come over the hills in his bag, and he had come to think of it less as money than as a letter that happened to weigh something. He ran the figures through anyway, every time, because a sum you had checked was worth twice one you had assumed.
